  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday morning through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1220 PM EST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nThe long term period opens on Friday morning with an Arctic surface \nhigh beginning to dive southward across central CONUS, accompanied \nby a steady stream of cold, dry continental air at the mid-levels. \nThe combination of the two -- and the aforementioned high pressure \nsystem settling across the Southeast -- will result in mostly clear \nand dry conditions, along with a marked drop in temperatures that \nwill linger through the weekend and into next week. We'll be breezy \nto round off the work week, with frequent gusts to 20 to 25 mph \npossible on Friday. Highs each day Friday through Monday will be in \nthe 50s to lower 60s for all but northeast Georgia, which will \nstruggle to get out of the 40s (and potentially in the mid-to-upper \n30s on Friday for those in valleys with cold air drainage). Mornings \nwill become progressively chillier through Sunday, when those \nventuring outside around daybreak will likely experience lows in the \nmid-20s to mid-30s. Areas along and north of the I-85 corridor are \nlikely to experience a hard freeze, and subsequent product issuance \nwill follow in future forecast packages. \n\nRain chances will remain negligible into Monday, when a mid-level \nshortwave approaches from the west. There is modest disagreement \namongst global models regarding positioning of this feature, which \nwould impact the north-south distribution of precipitation chances --\nleaning more GFS drags the trough across the northern Gulf, which \nwould relegate meaningful rain chances to our far southern tier. At \nany rate, as the lingering surface high is nudged into place across \nthe Mid-Atlantic on Monday, any associated moisture will overrun the \nresultant cool dome, which would result in reinforcement of the \npresiding cool, stable airmass.",
